  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Line \\(a\\) is parallel to plane \\(\\alpha\\). A plane containing \\(a\\) intersects \\(\\alpha\\) along \\(b\\). What follows?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(a\\) is perpendicular to \\(b\\)",
    "B": "\\(a\\) and \\(b\\) intersect",
    "C": "\\(a\\) is parallel to \\(b\\)",
    "D": "\\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are skew"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "This is a property of a line parallel to a plane: the line of intersection is parallel to the original line.\n\nAlternative method 1: Use coplanarity and nonintersection\n\nBoth \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) lie in the cutting plane, so they are coplanar. Since \\(a\\parallel\\alpha\\), no point of \\(a\\) lies in \\(\\alpha\\). But \\(b\\) lies in \\(\\alpha\\), so \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) have no common point. Coplanar lines with no common point are parallel. Thus \\(a\\parallel b\\), choice C.",
